    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct solution is <b>MongoDB Atlas with Charts</b> because it is fully managed, supports a multicloud strategy across the major providers, and delivers low latency for high volume device reads and writes.</p><p><b>MongoDB Atlas</b> runs on Google Cloud, AWS, and Azure and can span them with multi cloud and global clusters, which directly satisfies the multicloud requirement. It provides high throughput and low latency for IoT workloads with horizontal scaling and time series collections, so it can comfortably ingest and query telemetry from 60,000 sensors while keeping response times low.</p><p><b>MongoDB Charts</b> gives native visualization on top of the managed database, so the platform can analyze trends and dashboards without adding separate BI infrastructure, while the core device interactions continue to benefit from the low latency data plane in <b>Atlas</b>.</p><p><i>Cloud Spanner with Looker Studio</i> is fully managed and scales strongly, yet it is limited to Google Cloud and therefore does not meet a multicloud strategy. It is also a relational system which is not required for flexible IoT time series data.</p><p><i>Bigtable with Looker Studio</i> is an excellent fit for time series and offers very low latency at massive scale, but it is a Google Cloud only service and therefore does not satisfy the multicloud requirement in this scenario.</p><p><i>Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL with Looker Studio</i> is managed but is constrained to Google Cloud and typically cannot match the write throughput and connection scalability needed for tens of thousands of concurrent IoT devices compared to a distributed NoSQL service.</p>",
